      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nFile: `src/TokenWrapper.sol`\nFunction: `transferFrom(address from, address to, uint256 amount)`\nVulnerable Code:\n```solidity\nemit Transfer(msg.sender, to, amount);\n```\n\nThe `TokenWrapper` contract implements the IERC20 interface but contains a critical standard violation in its `transferFrom` function. When a user or contract calls `transferFrom` to move tokens on behalf of another address using an allowance, the emitted Transfer event incorrectly identifies the transaction initiator (msg.sender, the spender) as the source of the tokens, rather than the actual token holder (the from parameter) whose balance is being debited.\n\nAccording to the ERC-20 standard as defined in EIP-20, the Transfer event must emit the address of the token holder whose balance decreases as the from parameter. The current implementation violates this requirement by substituting msg.sender for the from address in the event emission. While the internal accounting through the _balanceOf mapping is updated correctly and the actual token balances reflect the proper transfer from the token holder to the recipient, the event log creates a permanent and incorrect record in the blockchain history.\n\nThis desynchronization between on-chain state and event emissions has cascading effects throughout the Ethereum ecosystem. Block explorers such as Etherscan and Blockscout rely on Transfer events to construct transaction histories and token movement visualizations. When these explorers parse the incorrect events from TokenWrapper, they will display the operator or router contract as the originating address rather than the actual token holder. Users checking their transaction history on these platforms will not see their TokenWrapper transfers in their sent transactions list, creating confusion and support burden.\n\nWallet applications including MetaMask, Rainbow, Coinbase Wallet, and others similarly depend on Transfer events to populate user transaction histories and portfolio tracking. Since the events incorrectly attribute transfers to the msg.sender rather than the token holder, users will not see accurate representations of their TokenWrapper activity within their wallet interfaces. This breaks the fundamental user experience expectation that wallet software accurately reflects token movements.\n\nOff-chain indexing systems face similar complications. The Graph subgraphs that index TokenWrapper transfers will store incorrect sender information, causing any dApps that query this data to display inaccurate information. Dune Analytics dashboards and queries that aggregate TokenWrapper volume will attribute trading activity to router contracts and operators rather than the actual users, making it impossible to accurately track user behavior or trading patterns. Tax reporting tools that scan blockchain events to generate transaction reports for users will fail to detect TokenWrapper transfers since the events do not identify the user as the sender, potentially causing incomplete tax reporting and compliance issues.\n\nThe impact extends to smart contract integrations as well. Protocols that listen to Transfer events for accounting purposes, such as DAO treasury tracking systems, token-gated access controls, referral tracking mechanisms, or rewards distribution contracts, will incorrectly credit activity to operator contracts rather than users. This breaks composability and makes TokenWrapper unsuitable for integration with other protocols that rely on standard-compliant event emissions.\n\nThe severity is classified as Medium because while the bug does not directly enable theft of funds or manipulation of on-chain balances, it fundamentally breaks the contract's compatibility with the Ethereum ecosystem. Every single `transferFrom` call is affected with one hundred percent likelihood, and the impact touches nearly every off-chain system and user interface in the ecosystem. The bug represents a complete violation of a widely-adopted standard that billions of dollars worth of infrastructure depends upon.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nThe fix requires a single line change in the TokenWrapper contract. In the `transferFrom` function of src/TokenWrapper.sol, modify the Transfer event emission to use the from parameter instead of msg.sender.",
      "proofOfConcept": "The following test demonstrates the vulnerability by showing that transferFrom emits the operator address instead of the token holder address in Transfer events. This test should be placed in a new file `test/TokenWrapperEventBug.t.sol` and can be executed with `forge test --match-test testTransferFromEventViolation -vvv`.\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity >=0.8.30;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {TokenWrapper} from \"../src/TokenWrapper.sol\";\nimport {Core} from \"../src/Core.sol\";\nimport {IERC20} from \"forge-std/interfaces/IERC20.sol\";\n\ncontract MockERC20 is IERC20 {\n    mapping(address => uint256) private _balances;\n    mapping(address => mapping(address => uint256)) private _allowances;\n    \n    function transfer(address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {\n        _balances[msg.sender] -= amount;\n        _balances[to] += amount;\n        emit Transfer(msg.sender, to, amount);\n        return true;\n    }\n    \n    function approve(address spender, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {\n        _allowances[msg.sender][spender] = amount;\n        emit Approval(msg.sender, spender, amount);\n        return true;\n    }\n    \n    function transferFrom(address from, address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {\n        _allowances[from][msg.sender] -= amount;\n        _balances[from] -= amount;\n        _balances[to] += amount;\n        emit Transfer(from, to, amount);\n        return true;\n    }\n    \n    function balanceOf(address account) external view returns (uint256) {\n        return _balances[account];\n    }\n    \n    function allowance(address owner, address spender) external view returns (uint256) {\n        return _allowances[owner][spender];\n    }\n    \n    function totalSupply() external pure returns (uint256) { return 0; }\n    function name() external pure returns (string memory) { return \"Mock Token\"; }\n    function symbol() external pure returns (string memory) { return \"MOCK\"; }\n    function decimals() external pure returns (uint8) { return 18; }\n    \n    function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external {\n        _balances[to] += amount;\n    }\n}\n\ncontract TokenWrapperEventBugTest is Test {\n    TokenWrapper wrapper;\n    MockERC20 underlyingToken;\n    Core core;\n    \n    address alice = address(0xAAAAAA);\n    address bob = address(0xBBBBBB);\n    address operator = address(0xCCCCCC);\n    \n    event Transfer(address indexed from, address indexed to, uint256 value);\n    \n    function setUp() public {\n        core = new Core();\n        underlyingToken = new MockERC20();\n        wrapper = new TokenWrapper(core, underlyingToken, block.timestamp + 365 days);\n        \n        vm.startPrank(alice);\n        vm.store(\n            address(wrapper),\n            keccak256(abi.encode(alice, uint256(1))),\n            bytes32(uint256(1000 ether))\n        );\n        wrapper.approve(operator, type(uint256).max);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n    }\n    \n    function testTransferFromEventViolation() public {\n        uint256 transferAmount = 100 ether;\n        \n        uint256 aliceBalanceBefore = wrapper.balanceOf(alice);\n        uint256 bobBalanceBefore = wrapper.balanceOf(bob);\n        \n        vm.startPrank(operator);\n        \n        vm.expectEmit(true, true, true, true);\n        emit Transfer(operator, bob, transferAmount);\n        \n        bool success = wrapper.transferFrom(alice, bob, transferAmount);\n        \n        vm.stopPrank();\n        \n        assertTrue(success, \"Transfer should succeed\");\n        \n        assertEq(\n            wrapper.balanceOf(alice),\n            aliceBalanceBefore - transferAmount,\n            \"Alice balance should decrease by transfer amount\"\n        );\n        \n        assertEq(\n            wrapper.balanceOf(bob),\n            bobBalanceBefore + transferAmount,\n            \"Bob balance should increase by transfer amount\"\n        );\n        \n        vm.recordLogs();\n        \n        vm.prank(operator);\n        wrapper.transferFrom(alice, bob, transferAmount);\n        \n        Vm.Log[] memory logs = vm.getRecordedLogs();\n        \n        bool foundTransferEvent = false;\n        address eventFrom;\n        address eventTo;\n        uint256 eventAmount;\n        \n        for (uint i = 0; i < logs.length; i++) {\n            if (logs[i].topics.length == 3 && \n                logs[i].topics[0] == keccak256(\"Transfer(address,address,uint256)\")) {\n                foundTransferEvent = true;\n                eventFrom = address(uint160(uint256(logs[i].topics[1])));\n                eventTo = address(uint160(uint256(logs[i].topics[2])));\n                eventAmount = abi.decode(logs[i].data, (uint256));\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        assertTrue(foundTransferEvent, \"Transfer event should be emitted\");\n        \n        console.log(\"\\n=== BUG DEMONSTRATION ===\");\n        console.log(\"Token holder (should be event 'from'):\", alice);\n        console.log(\"Operator (msg.sender):                 \", operator);\n        console.log(\"Recipient:                             \", bob);\n        console.log(\"\");\n        console.log(\"Event emitted 'from':                  \", eventFrom);\n        console.log(\"Event emitted 'to':                    \", eventTo);\n        console.log(\"\");\n        \n        if (eventFrom == operator && eventFrom != alice) {\n            console.log(\"BUG CONFIRMED:\");\n            console.log(\"- Transfer event uses msg.sender (operator) as 'from'\");\n            console.log(\"- Should use 'from' parameter (alice) instead\");\n            console.log(\"- This violates ERC-20 standard (EIP-20)\");\n            console.log(\"\");\n            console.log(\"Impact:\");\n            console.log(\"- Block explorers will show operator as sender\");\n            console.log(\"- Wallets won't show this in alice's transaction history\");\n            console.log(\"- Off-chain indexers will misattribute the transfer\");\n            console.log(\"- Smart contracts listening to events will credit wrong address\");\n        }\n        \n        assertEq(eventFrom, operator, \"Bug: Event emits operator instead of token holder\");\n        assertEq(eventTo, bob, \"Event 'to' parameter is correct\");\n        assertEq(eventAmount, transferAmount, \"Event amount is correct\");\n        \n        assertTrue(eventFrom != alice, \"Bug: Token holder address not in event\");\n    }\n    \n    function testComparisonWithStandardERC20() public {\n        underlyingToken.mint(alice, 1000 ether);\n        \n        vm.prank(alice);\n        underlyingToken.approve(operator, type(uint256).max);\n        \n        vm.recordLogs();\n        \n        vm.prank(operator);\n        underlyingToken.transferFrom(alice, bob, 100 ether);\n        \n        Vm.Log[] memory logs = vm.getRecordedLogs();\n        \n        address standardFrom;\n        for (uint i = 0; i < logs.length; i++) {\n            if (logs[i].topics.length == 3 && \n                logs[i].topics[0] == keccak256(\"Transfer(address,address,uint256)\")) {\n                standardFrom = address(uint160(uint256(logs[i].topics[1])));\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        console.log(\"\\n=== CORRECT ERC-20 BEHAVIOR ===\");\n        console.log(\"Standard ERC-20 Transfer event 'from':\", standardFrom);\n        console.log(\"Token holder (alice):                 \", alice);\n        console.log(\"\");\n        \n        assertEq(standardFrom, alice, \"Standard ERC-20 correctly emits token holder as 'from'\");\n        console.log(\"Standard ERC-20 implementation correctly emits 'from' parameter\");\n    }\n    \n    function testMultipleOperatorTransfers() public {\n        address router = address(0xDDDDDD);\n        \n        vm.prank(alice);\n        wrapper.approve(router, type(uint256).max);\n        \n        vm.recordLogs();\n        \n        vm.prank(operator);\n        wrapper.transferFrom(alice, bob, 50 ether);\n        \n        vm.prank(router);\n        wrapper.transferFrom(alice, bob, 30 ether);\n        \n        Vm.Log[] memory logs = vm.getRecordedLogs();\n        \n        uint256 transferCount = 0;\n        for (uint i = 0; i < logs.length; i++) {\n            if (logs[i].topics.length == 3 && \n                logs[i].topics[0] == keccak256(\"Transfer(address,address,uint256)\")) {\n                address eventFrom = address(uint160(uint256(logs[i].topics[1])));\n                \n                transferCount++;\n                console.log(\"\\nTransfer\", transferCount);\n                console.log(\"Event 'from' address:\", eventFrom);\n                console.log(\"Should be (alice):   \", alice);\n                \n                assertTrue(eventFrom != alice, \"Bug affects all transferFrom calls\");\n            }\n        }\n        \n        assertEq(transferCount, 2, \"Should have two transfer events\");\n        console.log(\"\\nAll transferFrom operations emit incorrect events\");\n    }\n}\n```",
